#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111838 :tutorial/content "When two lines meet at a point, they create two pairs of opposite angles. The angles in each pair are called **vertical angles**.\n\nVertical angles are *always* congruent.\n\n![](images/0d/0d5b2895ae18beb7242ef666af08e5b8e3490880de99aec8d993b02f31a9fcf4.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, $\\angle 1$ and $\\angle 3$ are vertical angles and are therefore congruent.\n\nLikewise, $\\angle 2$ and $\\angle 4$ are vertical angles and are therefore congruent.\n\nSo, we have $\\angle 1 \\cong \\angle 3$ and $\\angle 2 \\cong \\angle 4$." :tutorial/id #uuid "d4d9e056-1e55-54e4-a5e4-9aee258c4fb0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2179 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
